[Music]  due to the pandemic the long border  between the u.s and canada remains  closed to non-essential travel  but essential does describe the work of  a small army of canadian health care  workers  every day they cross into the u.s to  care for american patients in michigan  hospitals  and during covid some got infected  themselves in canada there's been a push  back from people who fear these health  care workers could bring the virus back  home  but adriana diaz spoke with two nurses  who say despite those challenges  they wouldn't have it any other way  detroit nurse brent gale's commute  typically starts at 6 a.m  and in another country i cross the  international border every day  so when someone here says they were late  because of traffic i'm like i came from  another country  he works at saint mary mercy livonia  hospital in metro detroit  but lives across the detroit river in  windsor  canada we are on riverside drive in  windsor ontario looking at detroit  at least 1500 canadians work in  healthcare in michigan  some drawn by more job opportunities do  your patients realize  how many canadian nurses there are  working here in detroit  no i don't think they do but uh they  tease and joke that you know  i'll say something it'll be a turn of  phrase and then oh you're canadian  so you get that you say a boat or  something like that apparently i do  this small army of canadians have risked  their lives  especially in the spring of 2020 when  covid  raged in the u.s and there were more  daily copa deaths in michigan  than in all of canada it sucked it was  it  it was really bad you'd see the wave  coming and you're like okay we survived  it and then that next wave was coming  again before you could catch your breath  i just remember thinking like what is  going on like we're running out of  ventilators like  everybody needs to be intubated  canadian lindsay lafleur has been an er  nurse at detroit's henry ford hospital  for nearly five  years she says last spring's surge is  still  vivid i just remember standing in the  middle of the department and just  looking around like  dumbfounded and sad it must have been  pretty overwhelming  i cried a lot yeah parker said good  morning  that kovit's worst in detroit she had a  newborn at home in canada  and considered walking away from nursing  how nervous were you about your baby  i was really nervous she was really  young so we had her sleeping  in a pack and play in our room and i was  sleeping in her room in a tent  because i wanted to like conceal myself  somehow  so why do you do it why do you continue  to come  over the international border to work  here in detroit with covet patients  i love working here i love detroit i  love the hospital that i work for  i love the people that i work with you  like detroiters i like detroiters  yeah it's not it's not a border it's  just a line we  cross and we're the same people you know  and i just think it would have been  horribly cowardly to yeah to abandon my  american cousins  but back home in canada there was some  resistance to their cross-border work  there was sort of a stigma attached to  it a stigma a little bit you know you're  carrying the disease back and forth  there was a call in our local paper to  have us  you know stop being allowed to go back  and forth across the border  i was feeling the stress from the  community people were like you know we  thank you for all that you do but at the  same time like don't come near me  both did contract covid gail when a  patient's intubation tube came loose  lefleur thinks she may have gotten it in  windsor luckily her daughter did not  they recovered and remain committed as  ever  handsome wow you're awesome  stop it you're making me blessed still  treating their patients with a smile  there's this perception of canadians you  know the canadian nice  stereotype yeah is that real and does  that help you at work  i think it's more politeness than nice  and it does help  and we 